WEBVTT

00:00:00.016 --> 00:00:04.272
PostgreSQL FILTER gives us another way to calculate conditional measures.

00:00:04.272 --> 00:00:11.072
Instead of placing a CASE expression inside COUNT, we write the aggregate and then specify which rows are

00:00:11.072 --> 00:00:12.907
allowed to contribute to that aggregate.

00:00:12.907 --> 00:00:17.402
Run the example and compare its output with the previous CASE version.

00:00:17.402 --> 00:00:20.807
The counts should express the same analytical idea.

00:00:20.807 --> 00:00:27.372
FILTER can be especially readable when a SELECT list contains several independent conditional counts or sums.

00:00:27.372 --> 00:00:33.392
If you are practicing in pgAdmin, copy both versions and compare them side by side.

00:00:33.392 --> 00:00:36.482
The goal is not to decide that one is always better.

00:00:36.482 --> 00:00:42.807
The goal is to recognize both patterns and choose the one that is clear and appropriate for the

00:00:42.807 --> 00:00:44.497
SQL system you are using.

00:00:44.497 --> 00:00:49.992
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:49.992 --> 00:00:53.957
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:53.957 --> 00:00:56.412
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:56.412 --> 00:01:01.542
In a real setting such as a dashboard summarizing admissions by department and month,

00:01:01.542 --> 00:01:07.482
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:01:07.482 --> 00:01:09.892
before discussing syntax.

00:01:09.892 --> 00:01:16.398
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
